<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04NP">Example <span data-tag="04NP">9.6.6.20</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an ordinary category. Then a morphism $f: A \rightarrow B$ is left orthogonal to a morphism $g: X \rightarrow Y$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ if and only if every lifting problem </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =40pt@R=40pt{ A \ar [d]^{f} \ar [r]^-{u_0} &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ B \ar [r]^-{\overline{u}} \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
<p> admits a <em>unique</em> solution: that is, there is a unique morphism $u: B \rightarrow X$ satisfying $u \circ f = u_0$ and $g \circ u = \overline{u}$. </p>
</article>
